A||||8||geyser||aftermarket ignition module||||5||
Z||000000||geyser||01-21-2001||10:35 PM||||Is anyone aware of an aftermarket ignition module that is an improvement over the OEM unit? Accel lists one for the Chevy 4.3 V6 that looks exactly like the Fiero one, but Accel does not list a Fiero part number. ||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||01-22-2001||08:40 AM||||it s the same thing.. i've got one in my GT and GT Bastard has one in his SE. Blue Streak also makes one (cheaper version of the MSD) oh and MSD make one too... <A HREF="http://www.summitracing.com" TARGET=_blank>www.summitracing.com</A>  <A HREF="http://www.jegs.com" TARGET=_blank>www.jegs.com</A>  for part numbers... ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Raydar||01-22-2001||09:53 AM||||Okay, would you recommend the aftermarket one?<BR>Everything that I've ever heard said that the GM module was more reliable than aftermarket.<BR>Or was that just the generic "house-brand" module (Sorensen, etc.) and not the "performance" brands like Accel or MSD?<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>88 T-top Formula<BR>88 soon-to-be-V6 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||01-22-2001||11:36 AM||||they seem ok.. there was NO HP increase but there was a $8-10 difference.. so i'd say stay with the stock. unless you just have t have a yellow, blue or red box you don't see. but i've never had any problems with them... and jeffs still riding on his.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||01-22-2001||08:12 PM||||The biggest enemy of the module, and the pickup, is heat. All of the modules have a problem with too much of it. Heat is the main reason Fiero V6 installations seem to eat modules and pickups. It's not so much an issue of who makes a better one. The problem affects them all.<P>I've been working on the thermal issues in the Fiero engine bay since I wrote the first article on cooling issues for my cave well over a year ago. Most of the stuff back there is operating in the top of it's temperature range most of the time.<P>When you are dealing with electronics there is a specification called T case. This is basically the temperature of the case of the transisitor or what have you. If you look at T case for most electronics, other than military spec parts, T case is usually about 180F max. (It is posible to build more tolerant parts but only to a point. This also make the parts way more expencive.)<P>Keep in mind, whatever temperature the case is, the inside of the part is hotter yet. If T case is running 180 the inside of the part can be 10-20 degrees more depending on how the part is made.<P>Fiero V6 easily keeps the module case temperature at or even above the recomended range of most electronics parts. (Distributor 4's aren't quite as bad due to the location of the parts. DIS is hot but it seems to be built for it. It's rare to cook a DIS module.)<P>There are 3 things you can do to reduce the heat.<P>1. increase ventilation. Allot of heat is pooling at the top of the engine bay with little or no way out. The V6 distributor is right in the middle of it.<BR>2. reduce convected heat from the exhaust. (Coat the exhaust with Jet Hot or Techline products.)<BR>3. install better/more heat shielding to prevent IR heating of the distributor by the exhaust. (This has to be done carefully or you could create other problems.)||||reg||1
Z||000005||1FieroGTRacingInc||01-23-2001||09:01 PM||||What about using *Thermo-Tec* exhaust wrap on the manifold's???  I had that on my 87GT's manifold's..  It did seem to keep some of the heat down in the engine bay itself.....  Any opinion's on this Thermo-Tec wrap??  Anyone else using it on there exhaust anywhere???   Thank's   Steve<P>------------------<BR>1FieroGTRacingInc.||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||01-23-2001||09:18 PM||||If you've used it before then you likely know more than most of us. Anything that keeps heat down in the engine bay is a plus. <P>I personally don't like the wraps because the ones I've seen are nasty to work around and they are dificult or imposible to keep clean. I won't use them for this alone.<P>Another drawback is the installetion itself. getting it even and keeping it that way isn't fun. Then you have to fight with it every time you change a spark plug and other such things.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||01-23-2001||10:37 PM||||Here's my experience with Thermotac wrap. The engine compartment of my Lambo clone was extremely hot, so hot it would cause the fuel to boil in the lines and create a vaporlock type situation. The headers were coated with aluminum by my father who works for a metalic coating company. When I wrapped the headers, they got so hot that the aluminum coating melted and squeezed through the holes in the fiberglass wrap. There were little drips of aluminum hanging all over the headers! The stock ignition module and coil never failed.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/engine_1.jpg"> <BR>The wrap looks silver because I painted it with VHT Flameproof coating. I didn't like the brown color.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||1FieroGTRacingInc||01-23-2001||10:39 PM||||theogre....   I painted the wrap with a 1800* BLACK paint I found at *Prep Boy's*...  $7.99 a can...  It took almost BOTH can's, There pretty seal now, grease just wipes off with a wipe with a good rag!!! They are alway's black and look real clean,lol, most of the time!!!! This is jusy my opinion....  everybody know about *opinion's*....   they are ALL different... some are anyway's...   I Better stop here, because I feel like I'm starting toARGUE  with myself on this one...  I'm SOOOO Tired, as you can tell...  LOL  Steve...<P>------------------<BR>1FieroGTRacingInc.||||reg||1
